      "id": "2108010747838320819",
      "public_metrics": {
        "bookmark_count": 0,
        "impression_count": 12,
        "like_count": 1,
        "quote_count": 0,
        "reply_count": 1,
        "retweet_count": 0
      },
      "text": "Explainer: detention vs demurrage. They get conflated on invoices but are different clocks. Demurrage: a loaded container sits inside the terminal past its last free day. Detention: the container is outside the terminal past free time and the empty hasn't been returned. (1/2)",
      "url": "https://x.com/CandCWarehouse/status/2108010747838320819"
    },
    {
      "author_id": "1990248422289629185",
      "created_at": "2026-10-26T01:38:15.000Z",
      "edit_history_tweet_ids": [
        "2114532024056713352"
      ],
      "id": "2114532024056713352",
      "in_reply_to_user_id": "1990248422289629185",
      "public_metrics": {
        "bookmark_count": 0,
        "impression_count": 8,
        "like_count": 0,
        "quote_count": 0,
        "reply_count": 1,
        "retweet_count": 0
      },
      "referenced_tweets": [
        {
          "id": "2114532022844850568",
          "type": "replied_to"
        }
      ],
      "text": "Demurrage runs from discharge until the container gates out. Detention runs from gate-out until the empty is returned. One box can accrue both in sequence. Trucker detention is a third thing: a driver waiting at your dock past free time, billed hourly. (2/3)",
      "url": "https://x.com/CandCWarehouse/status/2114532024056713352"
    },
